WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.250
The book's decision matrix runs
on one rule: one decider per row.

2
00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:07.656
The single deliberate
exception is the pilot gate,

3
00:00:07.656 --> 00:00:10.575
G2, which the steward
and the sponsor co-sign.

4
00:00:10.575 --> 00:00:15.753
The sponsor and the CIO own scope, funding
and sequence, break ties, and decide

5
00:00:15.753 --> 00:00:21.425
when Active Directory components retire,
because each retirement ends a fallback.

6
00:00:21.425 --> 00:00:26.425
Their job is to keep this one program, not
five projects that finish one at a time.

7
00:00:26.425 --> 00:00:29.287
The CISO decides the control requirements.

8
00:00:29.287 --> 00:00:34.440
The authorizing official is the only one
who can accept risk on the agency's behalf,

9
00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:39.516
so every place the program carries risk
instead of removing it reaches that desk:

10
00:00:39.516 --> 00:00:42.167
a deferred legacy-authentication cutoff,

11
00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:46.713
a Kerberos island with no end date, a
deviation from a mandatory baseline.

12
00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:49.837
The steward's program
office runs the orchestra,

13
00:00:49.837 --> 00:00:52.024
but it doesn't decide risk or scope.

14
00:00:52.024 --> 00:00:55.139
Application owners decide
for their applications,

15
00:00:55.139 --> 00:00:57.963
and HR decides what the
worker attributes mean.

16
00:00:57.963 --> 00:01:02.979
The sponsor's decision, before the program
starts: fill in the matrix with names,

17
00:01:02.979 --> 00:01:05.757
one decider per row,
and get the authorizing

18
00:01:05.757 --> 00:01:08.999
official's agreement to
every risk-acceptance row.

19
00:01:08.999 --> 00:01:12.720
Next, three decisions that
often land on the wrong desk.
